+
+### [Procedural Ray/Box Intersection](8356-procedural-ray-box-intersection.md)
+Determines ray entry and exit within a volume's bounding box entirely within the fragment shader, without pre-rendering geometry. It computes the ray's intersection with the bounding box directly in the shader, allowing the volume to be rendered as a simple screen-aligned quad or similar primitive. Unlike rasterization-based setup methods that use geometry to pass ray data, this approach is self-contained but places the full computational load of intersection and marching on the fragment shader.
 
 ### Clipping Cell-Level Slice to Active Rays
-#### definition
-An optimization to reduce cell primitives in a slice by intersecting individual rays with the world-space bounding box of the current u,v slice [S1, unofficial].
-#### step
-Ignore rays that have already successfully hit a cell (inactive rays), even if they intersect the slice bounding box [S1, unofficial].
-
-### Procedural Ray/Box Intersection
-#### definition
-A method for determining ray entry and exit within the volume bounding box entirely within the fragment shader. The ray is defined by the camera position and the volume entry position [S2, unofficial].
-#### parameter
-Requires an exit criterion to determine when the ray has left the volume [S2, unofficial].
-#### pitfall
-The entire computational load for intersection and marching is placed on the fragment shader, which is a disadvantage compared to rasterization-based setup [S2, unofficial].
+An optimization to reduce cell primitives in a slice by intersecting individual rays with the world-space bounding box of the current u,v slice [S1, unofficial]. Rays that have already successfully hit a cell are considered "inactive" and are ignored, even if they intersect the slice bounding box [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The analytical slab method for AABB intersection is a foundational technique in ray tracing and volume rendering, widely implemented in graphics hardware and software renderers. The face distance method appears in early interactive volume rendering literature for determining entry points into rectangular data volumes. Ray filtering using AABBs and alpha masks is a standard optimization in modern GPU-based volume raycasting to cull empty space.
+The slab method for AABB intersection is a standard technique in ray tracing, widely cited in foundational graphics literature [S2, S3, S4, S6, S16, S22, S25, S30, S34, S43, S46, S53, unofficial][S56][S57, unofficial]. The face distance method appears in early interactive volume rendering contexts [S1, unofficial]. Ray filtering techniques are employed in modern GPU-based volume renderers to optimize batch processing [S1, S2, unofficial]. Procedural intersection in shaders is used in real-time rendering to avoid geometry overhead [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
